St Margaret Clitherow Catholic Primary School
Jubilee 2025 – Pilgrims of Hope: Retrospective and Legacy
The Jubilee Year 2025, Pilgrims of Hope, was a significant and memorable time in the life of our Catholic school. Responding to Pope Francis’ invitation to live with “deep faith, lively hope and active charity,” our whole school community reflected on what it means to be people of hope and, importantly, how we can make that hope visible through our actions.
Throughout the Holy Year, pupils, staff and families were encouraged to recognise the hope that comes from knowing that we are loved by God and to share that hope with others. Through prayer and worship, Religious Education, charitable action and the wider Catholic life and mission of our school, we explored how we can be signs of hope for others, particularly those who are vulnerable, marginalised, lonely or in need.
The Jubilee strengthened our understanding that Christian hope calls us to action. Inspired by Jesus’ message of love, justice, freedom and peace, our children were encouraged to recognise injustice, value the dignity of every person, care for those in need and take responsibility for our common home. They were given opportunities to see that their own choices and actions, however small, can make a positive difference.
Our journey as Pilgrims of Hope also reminded us that pilgrimage is something we undertake together. Shared opportunities for prayer, reflection, celebration, service and action strengthened the bonds between pupils, staff, families, our parish and the wider community, while helping us recognise that we are part of the universal Church.
The Legacy of the Jubilee
Although the Holy Year has ended, our journey as Pilgrims of Hope continues. Jubilee 2025 was not simply a theme for one year, but an opportunity to deepen our understanding of our mission as a Catholic school and how we are called to live out the Gospel each day.
Its legacy will be reflected in our continuing commitment to:
place Christ at the centre of our school community and nurture faith, hope and love;
ensure that every child knows that they are valued, loved and that they belong;
provide meaningful opportunities for prayer, worship and spiritual development;
enable pupils to become active participants in the Catholic life and mission of the school;
encourage pupil leadership and give children opportunities to put their faith into action;
stand alongside those who are vulnerable, marginalised or experiencing injustice;
promote racial justice, equality, dignity and respect for every member of the human family;
care for our common home through our continuing commitment to Laudato Si’ and environmental stewardship;
strengthen our relationships with families, our parish and the wider community; and
help our children understand that they have both the voice and the ability to make a positive difference in the world.
Above all, the lasting legacy of Jubilee 2025 will be our determination to be a community that does not simply talk about hope, but actively lives it. We will continue our pilgrimage together, encouraging our children to recognise opportunities to become signs of hope and to play their part in building God’s kingdom of love, justice and peace.
